{
  "card": {
    "agent_id": "c15-volatility-wide",
    "display_name": "C15 - Volatility Shock Reversal: leveraged financials & cyclicals",
    "discipline": "volatility",
    "thesis": "Mechanism: a single-name true-range day at least 1.5x its own ATR(14), closing down (close<open) and already oversold (rsi(14)<30), is a volatility shock -- forced or reflexive selling that has temporarily overwhelmed the name's normal liquidity, not (in most cases) a full repricing of fair value in one session. The counterparty on the other side of my entry is whoever sold into that shock day at the worst print of the move: margin-called leveraged longs, risk-parity/vol-targeting funds cutting gross exposure mechanically as realized vol jumps, and short-covering-driven air pockets on the way down. None of those sellers are pricing the business; they are meeting a risk constraint. I am buying from them at the next session's open (the broker's fill convention) and selling into the reflex bounce, via crosses_above sma(10), a short stop, or a 10-day time cap if neither arrives. This is a short-horizon, volatility-triggered reversal, not a fundamental dip-buy -- the entry gate is defined entirely by the magnitude of the move (ATR multiple) rather than by trend context, which is what makes it a volatility-discipline card rather than a mean-reversion one built on RSI level alone. Falsification: if the shock is not noise but new, correctly-priced information -- a name is genuinely insolvent, guidance is permanently impaired, a business model is broken -- the 'bounce' will not arrive before the stop or the time cap, and the strategy just bought a falling knife repeatedly. That is precisely what I expect happens in gfc: several of these financials (C, BAC, AIG, MS, GS, WFC) had genuinely impaired balance sheets in 2008, not merely a liquidity air-pocket, so the reversal mechanism should fail hardest exactly there, which is why I name gfc as my predicted worst window rather than treating the mechanism as universal. Selection and index status: this universe is NOT index-derived. I did not draw it from data/universe/index-constituents.json and I am not relying on any 'these are today's S&P 500 members' fact. I chose money-center banks, a broker-dealer pair, an insurer, an auto manufacturer and a miner because balance-sheet leverage and operating/commodity cyclicality are structural, multi-decade properties of these business types (a levered bank's equity is a leveraged claim on assets, so its volatility is structurally higher than an unlevered industrial's, in any decade) and because I required, before looking at any trading-window return, (a) price history to at least 2000 so the card can be judged in gfc, covid, q4-2018 and both chop windows, and (b) trailing-year ATR/price ratios through 2026-02-13 clustered tightly enough that one stop-loss and one position size are a defensible approximation across the whole book (see measurements below) -- the same non-window-scanning selection discipline the prior round's G-volatility card used, applied here to a sector nobody else in this round's field has touched (every other card I found in data/rounds/r3-2026-08/cards -- B-wide, C-wide, c12, c13, c14, c16, c17 -- is built on AI/semis/cloud names, staples/utilities, or a Treasury/gold flight-to-quality basket). I explicitly did not use my knowledge of which of these names crashed hardest in 2008 to choose among them -- the shortlist I scored (C, BAC, MS, GS, AIG, WFC, F, FCX, OXY, SLB, JPM, COF, BIIB, REGN, MRVL) is a structural sector list scored on ATR/price and history length alone, and the 10 finalists are simply the subset with the tightest dispersion cluster, not the subset with the biggest 2008 drawdown.",
